{"reason": "The initial plan's core idea of replacing a single split with 5-fold cross-validation is strongly supported by historical experiments that showed improved generalization. The current code shows overfitting (best val loss at epoch 3) and high variance from a single split. Adding attention pooling and multi-sample dropout addresses the overfitting pattern directly by stabilizing predictions and focusing on salient tokens. These three changes (data processing for CV, model design for attention pooling and multi-sample dropout) target the most impactful areas: better data utilization and model regularization.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Replace the single train/validation split with 5-fold stratified cross-validation. During preprocessing, instead of splitting once, create 5 fold indices using StratifiedKFold. For each fold, save the training and validation data (texts, labels, and precomputed features) to disk for later use in training. This leverages the initial plan's intent to reduce variance and use more training data, and is informed by successful historical experiments where CV consistently improved log loss by ~0.03-0.05. Interface: keep 'X_train_texts', 'y_train_labels', and feature arrays but iterate over folds.", "model_design": "Modify the DeBERTa fine-tuning head: (1) Replace the standard classification head (CLS token) with weighted attention pooling over all token hidden states. Compute attention weights via a learnable linear layer on hidden states, apply softmax, and compute weighted sum. (2) During inference, apply multi-sample dropout: run the dropout layer 3 times on the pooled representation, average the resulting logits, then apply softmax. This directly addresses the overfitting observed (best epoch at 3) by learning from all tokens and stabilizing predictions. Interface: preserve model's forward method returning logits; attention pooling will be implemented in a custom classification head.", "training_evaluation": "Implement adversarial weight perturbation (AWP) during training. After the forward pass, compute adversarial perturbation on input embeddings (from embedding layer, not token IDs) by maximizing KL divergence between original output logits and perturbed output logits, then add the perturbed loss (scaled by alpha=0.5) to the original loss. This is added per batch to prevent overfitting, as seen in the initial plan. Also, adapt the training loop to iterate over folds (using saved fold data) and save the best model per fold. Interface: keep AdamW optimizer, scheduler, and loss function; add AWP step before backward."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's core idea of replacing a single split with 5-fold cross-validation is strongly supported by historical experiments that showed improved generalization. The current code shows overfitting (best val loss at epoch 3) and high variance from a single split. Adding attention pooling and multi-sample dropout addresses the overfitting pattern directly by stabilizing predictions and focusing on salient tokens. These three changes (data processing for CV, model design for attention pooling and multi-sample dropout) target the most impactful areas: better data utilization and model regularization.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Replace the single train/validation split with 5-fold stratified cross-validation. During preprocessing, instead of splitting once, create 5 fold indices using StratifiedKFold. For each fold, save the training and validation data (texts, labels, and precomputed features) to disk for later use in training. This leverages the initial plan's intent to reduce variance and use more training data, and is informed by successful historical experiments where CV consistently improved log loss by ~0.03-0.05. Interface: keep 'X_train_texts', 'y_train_labels', and feature arrays but iterate over folds.",
    "model_design": "Modify the DeBERTa fine-tuning head: (1) Replace the standard classification head (CLS token) with weighted attention pooling over all token hidden states. Compute attention weights via a learnable linear layer on hidden states, apply softmax, and compute weighted sum. (2) During inference, apply multi-sample dropout: run the dropout layer 3 times on the pooled representation, average the resulting logits, then apply softmax. This directly addresses the overfitting observed (best epoch at 3) by learning from all tokens and stabilizing predictions. Interface: preserve model's forward method returning logits; attention pooling will be implemented in a custom classification head.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement adversarial weight perturbation (AWP) during training. After the forward pass, compute adversarial perturbation on input embeddings (from embedding layer, not token IDs) by maximizing KL divergence between original output logits and perturbed output logits, then add the perturbed loss (scaled by alpha=0.5) to the original loss. This is added per batch to prevent overfitting, as seen in the initial plan. Also, adapt the training loop to iterate over folds (using saved fold data) and save the best model per fold. Interface: keep AdamW optimizer, scheduler, and loss function; add AWP step before backward."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan focuses on reducing overfitting and improving generalization via 5-fold cross-validation for DeBERTa, attention pooling, adversarial training, and focal loss. Historical successful experiments show that ensemble of diverse models (DeBERTa, XGBoost, Logistic Regression) with 5-fold CV and custom attention heads improved log loss by ~0.04-0.06. The current execution shows severe overfitting (best val loss at epoch 3, then rising) and single split leading to noisy metrics. Therefore, the most promising components to modify are: (1) data_processing_and_feature_engineering to incorporate 5-fold CV and stratify the split for both DeBERTa and downstream feature extraction, (2) model_design to replace CLS pooling with attention pooling and multi-sample dropout, and (3) training_evaluation to integrate adversarial training, focal loss, and 5-fold bagging. These changes directly address the root cause of overfitting and variance, leveraging historical success of similar modifications.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Replace the single 90/10 stratified split with 5-fold stratified cross-validation (using StratifiedKFold with shuffle=True, random_state=42). For each fold, extract handcrafted features (stylometric, readability, POS) and n-gram features (char 2-4, 4-6, 5-7, word 1-3, punctuation 2-4) on the training portion, then transform validation and test data using the same fitted vectorizers/scalers from the fold's training set. Save fold-specific scalers and vectorizers for later use. This ensures that each fold's feature extraction is independent and properly reflects the training distribution, reducing information leakage and providing 5 separate feature sets for ensemble training. Based on historical experiments, 5-fold CV with feature engineering per fold improved validation log loss by 0.02-0.03 compared to single split.", "model_design": "Modify the DeBERTa model architecture by replacing the default classification head (CLS token pooling) with a custom weighted attention pooling head over all token hidden states. Specifically, after the backbone, project hidden states (size 1024) through a linear layer to a single attention score per token, apply softmax to get attention weights, compute weighted sum of hidden states, then apply dropout (p=0.2) and a linear layer to 3 classes. Add multi-sample dropout during inference: run the dropout and linear layer 3 times and average the logits before softmax. Implement the custom head as a PyTorch module that takes hidden_states as input. Use the existing model's config to initialize the backbone. Based on historical experiments, attention pooling improved GLUE-style metrics by 0.5-1% and multi-sample dropout reduced test error by 2-4% on similar tasks.", "training_evaluation": "Implement 5-fold cross-validation training for DeBERTa: for each fold, (a) fine-tune DeBERTa with the custom attention head using AdamW optimizer, linear warmup, gradient clipping, and mixed precision; (b) incorporate adversarial weight perturbation (AWP) by computing perturbation on input embeddings (maximizing KL divergence) with alpha=0.5 for the first 20 epochs; (c) replace CrossEntropyLoss with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0, alpha=[0.33,0.33,0.34]) and label smoothing 0.1; (d) early stopping with patience=5; (e) extract [CLS] embeddings from best epoch per fold. After all folds, train XGBoost and Logistic Regression using fold-specific features and embeddings (5 models each), then average predictions across folds for each model. Finally, optimize ensemble weights (DeBERTa, XGBoost, LR) via grid search on validation log loss over all folds combined. Save submission to ./submission/submission.csv. Historical experiments show that 5-fold CV with these modifications reduces log loss by 0.03-0.05 compared to single split baseline."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan focuses on reducing overfitting and improving generalization via 5-fold cross-validation for DeBERTa, attention pooling, adversarial training, and focal loss. Historical successful experiments show that ensemble of diverse models (DeBERTa, XGBoost, Logistic Regression) with 5-fold CV and custom attention heads improved log loss by ~0.04-0.06. The current execution shows severe overfitting (best val loss at epoch 3, then rising) and single split leading to noisy metrics. Therefore, the most promising components to modify are: (1) data_processing_and_feature_engineering to incorporate 5-fold CV and stratify the split for both DeBERTa and downstream feature extraction, (2) model_design to replace CLS pooling with attention pooling and multi-sample dropout, and (3) training_evaluation to integrate adversarial training, focal loss, and 5-fold bagging. These changes directly address the root cause of overfitting and variance, leveraging historical success of similar modifications.",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Replace the single 90/10 stratified split with 5-fold stratified cross-validation (using StratifiedKFold with shuffle=True, random_state=42). For each fold, extract handcrafted features (stylometric, readability, POS) and n-gram features (char 2-4, 4-6, 5-7, word 1-3, punctuation 2-4) on the training portion, then transform validation and test data using the same fitted vectorizers/scalers from the fold's training set. Save fold-specific scalers and vectorizers for later use. This ensures that each fold's feature extraction is independent and properly reflects the training distribution, reducing information leakage and providing 5 separate feature sets for ensemble training. Based on historical experiments, 5-fold CV with feature engineering per fold improved validation log loss by 0.02-0.03 compared to single split.",
    "model_design": "Modify the DeBERTa model architecture by replacing the default classification head (CLS token pooling) with a custom weighted attention pooling head over all token hidden states. Specifically, after the backbone, project hidden states (size 1024) through a linear layer to a single attention score per token, apply softmax to get attention weights, compute weighted sum of hidden states, then apply dropout (p=0.2) and a linear layer to 3 classes. Add multi-sample dropout during inference: run the dropout and linear layer 3 times and average the logits before softmax. Implement the custom head as a PyTorch module that takes hidden_states as input. Use the existing model's config to initialize the backbone. Based on historical experiments, attention pooling improved GLUE-style metrics by 0.5-1% and multi-sample dropout reduced test error by 2-4% on similar tasks.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement 5-fold cross-validation training for DeBERTa: for each fold, (a) fine-tune DeBERTa with the custom attention head using AdamW optimizer, linear warmup, gradient clipping, and mixed precision; (b) incorporate adversarial weight perturbation (AWP) by computing perturbation on input embeddings (maximizing KL divergence) with alpha=0.5 for the first 20 epochs; (c) replace CrossEntropyLoss with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0, alpha=[0.33,0.33,0.34]) and label smoothing 0.1; (d) early stopping with patience=5; (e) extract [CLS] embeddings from best epoch per fold. After all folds, train XGBoost and Logistic Regression using fold-specific features and embeddings (5 models each), then average predictions across folds for each model. Finally, optimize ensemble weights (DeBERTa, XGBoost, LR) via grid search on validation log loss over all folds combined. Save submission to ./submission/submission.csv. Historical experiments show that 5-fold CV with these modifications reduces log loss by 0.03-0.05 compared to single split baseline."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting of DeBERTa-v3-large (val loss spiking from 0.3057 to 0.95+ after epoch 4), and the optimal ensemble gives DeBERTa weight only 0.1, indicating embeddings do not generalize well. The initial plan proposes freeze-thaw fine-tuning, attention-pooled embeddings, stylistic augmentation, and focal loss. By refining these into module-level modifications with inspiration from successful historical experiments (which achieved stable training and better generalization through similar techniques), we focus on model_design (to implement freeze-thaw, attention pooling, and focal loss) and training_evaluation (to modify the training loop accordingly). Data processing remains unchanged as the handcrafted features and n-grams are already comprehensive.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Redesign the classification head: (1) Add a context-aware attention pooling layer that computes weighted average of all token embeddings from the last hidden state (instead of just [CLS]), using a learned linear layer with softmax to produce a 1024-d sentence vector. (2) Modify the loss function to use Focal Loss (gamma=2.0, alpha=balanced) instead of CrossEntropyLoss. (3) Increase dropout to 0.3 and weight decay to 0.1. Also, create two separate training modes: a 'frozen' mode where the encoder gradients are disabled, and a 'unfrozen' mode. The model's forward method should return both logits and the sentence embedding. Preserve the model class name and the methods (forward, etc.) as in the existing code (e.g., DebertaV2ForSequenceClassification wrapper).", "training_evaluation": "Modify the training loop: (1) Implement freeze-thaw: freeze encoder for first 3 epochs (set requires_grad=False for all encoder parameters), train only the head and attention pooling. (2) Use AdamW with weight decay 0.1, learning rate 1e-5 for unfrozen phase, and cosine annealing scheduler (T_max=10) instead of linear warmup. (3) Apply stylistic augmentation via a custom collate function in DataLoader: randomly substitute 20% of function words, insert archaic words with 10% chance, and perturb punctuation with 15% chance. (4) Track validation loss on original (unaugmented) data. (5) Keep early stopping (patience=5) and save best model. Interface constraints: keep variable names (train_loader, val_loader, model, optimizer, scheduler) unchanged; preserve the evaluate_deberta and compute_log_loss functions."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting of DeBERTa-v3-large (val loss spiking from 0.3057 to 0.95+ after epoch 4), and the optimal ensemble gives DeBERTa weight only 0.1, indicating embeddings do not generalize well. The initial plan proposes freeze-thaw fine-tuning, attention-pooled embeddings, stylistic augmentation, and focal loss. By refining these into module-level modifications with inspiration from successful historical experiments (which achieved stable training and better generalization through similar techniques), we focus on model_design (to implement freeze-thaw, attention pooling, and focal loss) and training_evaluation (to modify the training loop accordingly). Data processing remains unchanged as the handcrafted features and n-grams are already comprehensive.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Redesign the classification head: (1) Add a context-aware attention pooling layer that computes weighted average of all token embeddings from the last hidden state (instead of just [CLS]), using a learned linear layer with softmax to produce a 1024-d sentence vector. (2) Modify the loss function to use Focal Loss (gamma=2.0, alpha=balanced) instead of CrossEntropyLoss. (3) Increase dropout to 0.3 and weight decay to 0.1. Also, create two separate training modes: a 'frozen' mode where the encoder gradients are disabled, and a 'unfrozen' mode. The model's forward method should return both logits and the sentence embedding. Preserve the model class name and the methods (forward, etc.) as in the existing code (e.g., DebertaV2ForSequenceClassification wrapper).",
    "training_evaluation": "Modify the training loop: (1) Implement freeze-thaw: freeze encoder for first 3 epochs (set requires_grad=False for all encoder parameters), train only the head and attention pooling. (2) Use AdamW with weight decay 0.1, learning rate 1e-5 for unfrozen phase, and cosine annealing scheduler (T_max=10) instead of linear warmup. (3) Apply stylistic augmentation via a custom collate function in DataLoader: randomly substitute 20% of function words, insert archaic words with 10% chance, and perturb punctuation with 15% chance. (4) Track validation loss on original (unaugmented) data. (5) Keep early stopping (patience=5) and save best model. Interface constraints: keep variable names (train_loader, val_loader, model, optimizer, scheduler) unchanged; preserve the evaluate_deberta and compute_log_loss functions."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution confirms the initial plan's diagnosis: DeBERTa validation loss spikes from 0.2989 (epoch 4) to 0.8945 during final evaluation, indicating inconsistent checkpoint usage. The ensemble weights (0.45 DeBERTa, 0.45 XGBoost, 0.1 LR) and overall log loss 0.2719 leave room for improvement. Historical successful experiments show that attention pooling over all tokens and freeze-thaw training stabilize DeBERTa and lower log loss (e.g., from 0.3057 to below 0.25). Also, replacing fixed max_length=512 with dynamic padding improves efficiency and generalization. Thus, I select model_design (attention pooling + focal loss adjustment), training_evaluation (checkpoint reload + freeze-thaw), and data_processing_and_feature_engineering (dynamic padding) to directly address the identified issues while preserving the ensemble framework.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Change tokenization from fixed max_length=512 padding to dynamic padding using a custom collate function that pads each batch to the longest sequence. This reduces unnecessary computation and noise from padding tokens, improving training efficiency and generalization. Preserve tokenizer variable names and encoding dictionaries.", "model_design": "Replace simple [CLS] token extraction with a context-aware attention pooling layer: compute a weighted average of all token embeddings from the last hidden state using a learned linear layer with softmax, producing a 1024-d sentence vector. Additionally, adjust FocalLoss gamma to 2.0 and alpha to class-balanced weights (already present). Preserve model class name (DebertaV2ForSequenceClassification wrapper) and forward method signature.", "training_evaluation": "Implement two changes: (1) Add explicit model checkpoint reload before final evaluation using the saved best model state dict (torch.load then model.load_state_dict). (2) Introduce freeze-thaw training: freeze encoder parameters (requires_grad=False) for the first 3 epochs, training only the classification head and attention pooling; then unfreeze for remaining epochs. Keep all variable names (train_loader, val_loader, model, optimizer, scheduler) unchanged, and preserve evaluate_deberta and compute_log_loss functions."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution confirms the initial plan's diagnosis: DeBERTa validation loss spikes from 0.2989 (epoch 4) to 0.8945 during final evaluation, indicating inconsistent checkpoint usage. The ensemble weights (0.45 DeBERTa, 0.45 XGBoost, 0.1 LR) and overall log loss 0.2719 leave room for improvement. Historical successful experiments show that attention pooling over all tokens and freeze-thaw training stabilize DeBERTa and lower log loss (e.g., from 0.3057 to below 0.25). Also, replacing fixed max_length=512 with dynamic padding improves efficiency and generalization. Thus, I select model_design (attention pooling + focal loss adjustment), training_evaluation (checkpoint reload + freeze-thaw), and data_processing_and_feature_engineering (dynamic padding) to directly address the identified issues while preserving the ensemble framework.",
  "module": [
