    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Change tokenization from fixed max_length=512 padding to dynamic padding using a custom collate function that pads each batch to the longest sequence. This reduces unnecessary computation and noise from padding tokens, improving training efficiency and generalization. Preserve tokenizer variable names and encoding dictionaries.",
    "model_design": "Replace simple [CLS] token extraction with a context-aware attention pooling layer: compute a weighted average of all token embeddings from the last hidden state using a learned linear layer with softmax, producing a 1024-d sentence vector. Additionally, adjust FocalLoss gamma to 2.0 and alpha to class-balanced weights (already present). Preserve model class name (DebertaV2ForSequenceClassification wrapper) and forward method signature.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement two changes: (1) Add explicit model checkpoint reload before final evaluation using the saved best model state dict (torch.load then model.load_state_dict). (2) Introduce freeze-thaw training: freeze encoder parameters (requires_grad=False) for the first 3 epochs, training only the classification head and attention pooling; then unfreeze for remaining epochs. Keep all variable names (train_loader, val_loader, model, optimizer, scheduler) unchanged, and preserve evaluate_deberta and compute_log_loss functions."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show severe overfitting: training loss drops to 0.0774 while validation loss spikes to 0.8182, and the DeBERTa validation log loss at best epoch (0.3171) degrades later. The initial plan's Focal Loss and MixUp directly address this instability and overfitting. Historical similar experiments successfully used DeBERTa-v3-large with XGBoost ensemble achieving lower log loss, validating this architecture. I select model_design to implement Focal Loss for better handling of hard examples, and training_evaluation to add MixUp augmentation that regularizes the model and stabilizes training. data_processing_and_feature_engineering is not modified because the current feature extraction pipeline is well-established from historical successes and does not contribute to the core overfitting issue.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace CrossEntropyLoss with a robust Focal Loss function. Use gamma=2.0 and compute alpha weights from training class frequencies to emphasize minority classes. Implement as a custom loss class inheriting from nn.Module, accepting logits and targets, applying the focal scaling factor (1-p_t)^gamma. This directly addresses the validation instability documented in the initial plan by down-weighting well-classified examples and forcing the model to focus on difficult author distinctions. Interface: replace criterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss(label_smoothing=0.1) with criterion = FocalLoss(gamma=2.0, alpha=class_weights) where class_weights is a tensor of shape [3].", "training_evaluation": "Implement MixUp augmentation at the text level during training. For each batch, construct a random permutation of the batch; for each pair (x_i, x_p[i]), mix the token embeddings by interpolating input_ids (applying the same mix ratio to the full sequence) and linearly combining one-hot labels. Sample lambda from Beta(0.5,0.5). This acts as a strong regularizer to reduce overfitting, as shown by the initial plan's analysis. Integrate MixUp in the training loop after dataloader yields a batch: compute mixed inputs and mixed labels, then compute loss with the mixed labels using a custom mixup loss function. Preserve all validation and test interfaces unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show severe overfitting: training loss drops to 0.0774 while validation loss spikes to 0.8182, and the DeBERTa validation log loss at best epoch (0.3171) degrades later. The initial plan's Focal Loss and MixUp directly address this instability and overfitting. Historical similar experiments successfully used DeBERTa-v3-large with XGBoost ensemble achieving lower log loss, validating this architecture. I select model_design to implement Focal Loss for better handling of hard examples, and training_evaluation to add MixUp augmentation that regularizes the model and stabilizes training. data_processing_and_feature_engineering is not modified because the current feature extraction pipeline is well-established from historical successes and does not contribute to the core overfitting issue.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace CrossEntropyLoss with a robust Focal Loss function. Use gamma=2.0 and compute alpha weights from training class frequencies to emphasize minority classes. Implement as a custom loss class inheriting from nn.Module, accepting logits and targets, applying the focal scaling factor (1-p_t)^gamma. This directly addresses the validation instability documented in the initial plan by down-weighting well-classified examples and forcing the model to focus on difficult author distinctions. Interface: replace criterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss(label_smoothing=0.1) with criterion = FocalLoss(gamma=2.0, alpha=class_weights) where class_weights is a tensor of shape [3].",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement MixUp augmentation at the text level during training. For each batch, construct a random permutation of the batch; for each pair (x_i, x_p[i]), mix the token embeddings by interpolating input_ids (applying the same mix ratio to the full sequence) and linearly combining one-hot labels. Sample lambda from Beta(0.5,0.5). This acts as a strong regularizer to reduce overfitting, as shown by the initial plan's analysis. Integrate MixUp in the training loop after dataloader yields a batch: compute mixed inputs and mixed labels, then compute loss with the mixed labels using a custom mixup loss function. Preserve all validation and test interfaces unchanged."
  }
}"}

