{"reason": "The execution shows DeBERTa validation loss drastically higher (0.8945) when evaluated via evaluate_deberta compared to training best (0.2878), indicating a data mismatch from the label-inclusive loader. The ensemble validation log loss of 0.2714 is already strong, but the DeBERTa component is underutilized. Historical successful experiments used gradual unfreezing and discriminative learning rates to stabilize fine-tuning and prevent overfitting. The current overfitting (train loss 0.124 vs val 0.2878 by epoch 4) mirrors historical failures. Modifying training_evaluation with gradual unfreezing and discriminative learning rates directly addresses this, while model_design improvements to the classification head can better capture distributed authorial patterns. Data processing remains unchanged as features are sufficient.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the standard [CLS] classification head with a multi-head attention pooling layer that aggregates all token representations from the final hidden state, followed by a 2-layer MLP (intermediate dimension 512, dropout 0.2, LayerNorm) for classification. This captures distributed stylistic patterns across the sentence, which is beneficial for horror author attribution. The new head is initialized with small random weights and integrated into the model by overriding the classifier head. Preserve the backbone architecture (deberta-v3-large) and all forward pass logic.", "training_evaluation": "Implement two-phase training: Phase 1 (5 epochs) freezes the transformer backbone and trains only the new attention+MLP head with learning rate 5e-4, AdamW, weight decay 0.01, and linear warmup (10% of phase steps). Phase 2 unfreezes the entire model with discriminative learning rates: 1e-5 for head layers, 2e-5 for top encoder (last 4 layers), 5e-6 for mid encoder (layers 5-8), 2e-6 for bottom encoder (layers 1-4), using cosine annealing with restarts (T_0=5, T_mult=2) and early stopping (patience=3). Use the same CrossEntropyLoss with label_smoothing=0.1, gradient clipping max_norm=1.0, mixed precision. This stabilizes training and reduces overfitting as seen in historical successes."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution shows DeBERTa validation loss drastically higher (0.8945) when evaluated via evaluate_deberta compared to training best (0.2878), indicating a data mismatch from the label-inclusive loader. The ensemble validation log loss of 0.2714 is already strong, but the DeBERTa component is underutilized. Historical successful experiments used gradual unfreezing and discriminative learning rates to stabilize fine-tuning and prevent overfitting. The current overfitting (train loss 0.124 vs val 0.2878 by epoch 4) mirrors historical failures. Modifying training_evaluation with gradual unfreezing and discriminative learning rates directly addresses this, while model_design improvements to the classification head can better capture distributed authorial patterns. Data processing remains unchanged as features are sufficient.",
